# refuse() never returns, so no iterator ever reaches show(), which believes a string. The walk of walk() lays a check for a string in front of the call, which an iterator cannot pass,
# leaves never at show()'s parameter, and boxes the iterator behind the check. The judge of arrivals (findCallThatHandsOverWhatItsUnitHasNotLeft(), walk/AOTCompileUnit.cpp) excuses the
# call for that check, which stands in front of the box.
